  <qti-item-body>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="on" outcome-identifier="C_TEACH" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body>
        <h4>What It Means</h4>
        <p>Archaic means belonging to an earlier period of time and no longer in current use.</p>
        <h4>See the word in context</h4>
        <p><em>Much of his vocabulary felt <strong>archaic</strong>, so tied to his own century that Priya sometimes had to guess at his meaning.</em></p>
        <p><em>In her grandmother's attic, Maya found <strong>archaic</strong> letters written in a spidery hand, words she'd never seen before because the language itself had changed so much.</em></p>
        <p><strong>Archaic</strong> means something is not just old, but specifically old and no longer in use. It's more precise than "ancient" (which emphasizes age but not disuse) or "old" (which says nothing about whether the thing is still used). These letters belong to a vanished era of language — that's exactly what archaic captures.</p>
      </qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="on" outcome-identifier="C_Q1" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body>
        <qti-choice-interaction response-identifier="R1" shuffle="true" max-choices="1">
          <qti-prompt>Which sentence uses archaic correctly?</qti-prompt>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="A">My phone is only two years old, but it's completely archaic now that a newer model has come out.</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="B">The recipe measured everything in 'gills' and 'drams' — archaic units that no cook today would recognize or use.</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="C">The archaic stone bridge, built in 1750, still carries hundreds of cars into the village every single day.</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="D">Marco thinks wearing a wristwatch is archaic ever since smartwatches became slightly more popular.</qti-simple-choice>
        </qti-choice-interaction></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q1A" outcome-identifier="FB1" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>A two-year-old phone outclassed by a newer model is merely outdated. Archaic sets a higher bar: the thing must belong to a past era and have dropped completely out of daily life.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q1C" outcome-identifier="FB1" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Built in 1750, yes — but this bridge 'still carries hundreds of cars' every day, so it is very much in service. Archaic requires that something no longer be part of current life.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q1D" outcome-identifier="FB1" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Wristwatches are still on wrists everywhere — smartwatches being 'slightly more popular' is a fashion shift, not an extinction. Archaic is reserved for what genuinely belongs to a bygone era.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q1correct" outcome-identifier="FB1" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Exactly — measurement terms like gills and drams truly belong to an earlier era and have dropped out of use entirely, which is the full test archaic has to pass.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q1incorrect" outcome-identifier="FB1" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Archaic demands more than feeling dated: the thing must belong to a past era and be gone from everyday life. A two-year-old phone, a bridge still carrying daily traffic, and a wristwatch people still wear all fail that test. Find what has completely died out.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="on" outcome-identifier="C_Q2" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body>
        <p>The collegium's singers perform from three-hundred-year-old part-books whose pages crumble at a touch, so age alone rarely slows them down. But a twelfth-century choir manuscript defeated everyone: square marks sitting on a four-line frame, following rules that no member of the ensemble had ever been taught.</p>
        <p>A visiting specialist finally transcribed the archaic notation onto modern staff lines. Medieval singers once absorbed those square marks as naturally as children today absorb the treble clef, she noted, though centuries have passed since any working musician wrote music that way.</p>
        <qti-choice-interaction response-identifier="R2" shuffle="true" max-choices="1">
          <qti-prompt>As used in the text, what does the word "archaic" most nearly mean?</qti-prompt>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="A">very old and physically fragile</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="B">part of a whole system of music-writing that has since died out</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="C">too difficult for anyone but a specialist to master</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="D">slightly behind the latest fashion</qti-simple-choice>
        </qti-choice-interaction></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q2A" outcome-identifier="FB2" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>The opening sentence removes this reading: the ensemble already performs from crumbling three-hundred-year-old books without trouble, so neither age nor fragility is what the word is doing here.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q2C" outcome-identifier="FB2" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>The specialist points out that medieval singers absorbed these marks as naturally as children absorb the treble clef — the notation was never inherently hard. Ask instead why no one in the ensemble was ever taught it.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q2D" outcome-identifier="FB2" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>The scale is wrong. The passage measures the gap in centuries, not in seasons or styles; reread the final clause of the passage and weigh what it says about the last several centuries.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q2correct" outcome-identifier="FB2" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Right. Age is not the obstacle here — crumbling three-hundred-year-old part-books are routine for these singers. What stops them is notation whose rules nobody is taught anymore, because working musicians stopped writing with it centuries ago.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
  </qti-item-body>

